+section('git', 'Using git in a job', 'git')
  p.
    The Client doesn't clone anything itself and doesn't need git installed for its own sake. A job that needs a repository
    clones it in #[code --command], into its work directory (#[code $THUB_WORK_DIR], where the command starts), with the
    credentials passed as #[code --env]. #[code --env] values are masked everywhere and dropped when the job ends. Anything
    else (the URL, the command, #[code --meta]) is stored with the job and visible to anyone who can read it, so never put a
    token there.

  h3.h6 HTTPS with a token (recommended)
  +code('Token from your CI secret store').
    export GH_TOKEN=…      # GitHub: a fine-grained PAT with read access; GitLab: oauth2 token; Bitbucket: x-token-auth
    thub run --type sw \
      --env GH_TOKEN \
      --command 'git clone --depth 1 --branch main "https://x-access-token:$GH_TOKEN@github.com/yourorg/firmware-tests.git" . &&
                 ./ci/test.sh' \
      --wait
  p.small.
    The token appears only inside the command's environment. To keep it out of #[code .git/config] as well, pass it as a header:
    #[code git -c http.extraHeader="Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN" clone …].

  h3.h6 SSH with a key from the job
  +code('Private key as --env (base64), used for this job only').
    export DEPLOY_KEY_B64="$(base64 &lt; ~/.ssh/thub_deploy | tr -d '\n')"
    thub run --type hw --label board:nucleo-f401re \
      --env DEPLOY_KEY_B64 \
      --command 'umask 077 && echo "$DEPLOY_KEY_B64" | base64 -d > "$THUB_WORK_DIR/../key" &&
                 GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -i $THUB_WORK_DIR/../key -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new" \
                   git clone --depth 1 git@github.com:yourorg/firmware-tests.git . &&
                 ./ci/test.sh' \
      --wait
  p.small.
    The job directory, key included, is deleted when the job ends. Add the public key as a read-only deploy key: GitHub,
    Repository → Settings → Deploy keys; GitLab, Settings → Repository → Deploy keys; Bitbucket, Repository settings → Access keys.

  h3.h6 Branch, tag, commit, submodules, LFS
  +code('All in the command').
    git clone --depth 1 --branch v1.4.0 "$REPO" .                            # a branch or tag
    git init -q . && git remote add origin "$REPO" && git fetch -q --depth 1 origin a1b2c3d && git checkout -q FETCH_HEAD   # a commit
    git submodule update --init --recursive --depth 1 && git lfs pull        # submodules / LFS
  p.small.
    Pass the repository and ref as #[code --env REPO=…,REF=…] (or #[code --meta]) so the same command works for every branch.
    The exit code of #[code --command] is the verdict; JUnit files under #[code results/] or #[code artifacts/] are summed into
    the job's test counts. Nothing is uploaded: publish what you need to keep from the command (#[code curl -T …] with a token in #[code --env]).
